# Restrictive Unit Scopes

## Objective

Refactor unit scopes from a required inclusive selection into optional restrictions.
With no unit-scope restriction selected, SoulSearch starts from all active units.

Target behavior:

- No unit-scope restrictions means all active units are eligible.
- One restriction limits candidates to units matching that restriction.
- The state model is list-valued so a later phase can allow multiple selections.
- Until the separate multi-select task is implemented, the UI continues to select
  at most one restriction at a time.
- The primary window's existing first-open Humanoids race filter remains unchanged;
  therefore its effective initial results remain humanoids until that race filter is
  removed.
- `All units` is the presentation for an empty restriction list, not a persisted
  restriction alongside other scopes.

## Scope boundaries

- Included: provider semantics, state/config normalization, scoped-window callers,
  single-select UI presentation, compatibility handling, tests, and documentation.
- Excluded: allowing multiple simultaneous UI selections.
- Excluded: adding Wildlife, Livestock, or Pets scope predicates.
- Excluded: changing the primary window's first-open Humanoids race filter.
- Excluded: changing filter-preset persistence unless implementation reveals that
  presets already own unit-scope state.
- Do not hand-edit `dist/`; regenerate package output with the repository tooling.

## Phase 0 - Lock the contract and baseline

- [ ] Record a clean `./tools/Test.ps1` baseline in the execution evidence table.
- [ ] Add or confirm characterization tests for the current candidate pipeline:
  unit scope provider -> race filter provider -> resident row collection.
- [ ] Encode the target semantic contract in tests before changing production code:
  - empty restrictions return every active, unique unit in source order;
  - a single restriction returns only matching active units;
  - invalid restriction IDs are rejected or normalized at the documented boundary;
  - provider errors still return no partial candidate set.
- [ ] Confirm and document combination semantics for the future multi-select task:
  selected unit scopes will be ORed, while race filtering remains a downstream
  candidate restriction.
- [ ] Confirm compatibility policy for existing scalar callers:
  - `all_active` maps to an empty restriction list;
  - another valid scalar maps to a one-element list;
  - malformed values fall back to an empty restriction list in window config;
  - direct provider/session construction continues to reject unknown IDs.

Phase 0 gate:

- [ ] The baseline is green and tests state the new empty-means-all contract without
  yet requiring multi-select UI behavior.

## Phase 1 - Refactor the provider and protocol

- [ ] Update `filter_constants.lua` so the default unit-scope state is unrestricted.
- [ ] Decide whether `all_active` remains as a compatibility-only protocol value or
  is removed after all in-repo callers migrate; do not expose it as a combinable
  restriction.
- [ ] Replace the scalar provider input in `unit_scope_provider.lua` with normalized
  list/set input.
- [ ] Preserve active-unit checks, source ordering, duplicate suppression, and
  unavailable-context error propagation.
- [ ] Implement empty-list behavior as the all-active baseline.
- [ ] Implement one-restriction behavior through the existing scope predicates.
- [ ] Make the provider capable of ORing multiple restrictions without exposing
  multi-select UI yet.
- [ ] Return defensive copies from any exported scope-option/default APIs.
- [ ] Update `filter_constants_test.lua` and `unit_scope_provider_test.lua` for the
  new protocol and provider behavior.

Phase 1 gate:

- [ ] Focused provider/protocol tests pass, including empty, scalar-compatibility,
  one-item, multi-item OR, invalid, duplicate, inactive, and unavailable cases.

## Phase 2 - Migrate session state, configuration, and callers

- [ ] Replace singular `unit_scope` session state with list-valued `unit_scopes`.
- [ ] Add defensive copying/equality behavior so callers cannot mutate session or
  settings snapshots through returned tables.
- [ ] Replace scalar getters/setters with restriction-list operations appropriate for
  the current single-select UI.
- [ ] Update `window_config.lua` to normalize explicit options and saved session
  settings into restriction lists.
- [ ] Update `window_settings.lua` snapshots to store isolated restriction lists.
- [ ] Preserve compatibility for `ui.open({unit_scope=...})`; prefer the new
  `unit_scopes={...}` field when both are present and document the precedence.
- [ ] Update `creatures_menu_scope.lua` so Residents, Pets/Livestock, and Visitors
  scoped windows pass explicit one-element restriction lists.
- [ ] Update `main_window.lua` candidate refresh composition to pass the normalized
  restriction list into the unit-scope provider before race filtering.
- [ ] Update `tests/support/soulsearch_env.lua` stubs and fixtures to match the new
  state/API shape.
- [ ] Update `search_session_test.lua`, `window_config_test.lua`,
  `window_settings_test.lua`, `creatures_menu_scope_test.lua`, and `ui_open_test.lua`.

Phase 2 gate:

- [ ] Default, reopened, same-identity, and independently scoped windows retain the
  correct isolated restriction lists.
- [ ] Legacy scalar open options still resolve correctly without becoming the stored
  canonical representation.

## Phase 3 - Present restrictions in the existing single-select UI

- [ ] Display `Search for: All units` when the restriction list is empty.
- [ ] Treat choosing `All units` as clearing unit-scope restrictions.
- [ ] Keep choosing Citizens, Residents, Citizens and pets, or Visitors as a
  replacement operation so at most one restriction is selected in this task.
- [ ] Ensure the picker marks the active restriction and marks no concrete scope when
  the state is unrestricted.
- [ ] Update scope presenter/formatter APIs to consume restriction-list state.
- [ ] Update picker callbacks and annotations from scalar scope values to the new
  state contract.
- [ ] Decide and test whether `Clear filters` also clears unit-scope restrictions;
  preferred behavior is that it produces a genuinely unrestricted search.
- [ ] Decide and test whether the active filter count includes unit-scope
  restrictions; keep the label and tooltip behavior internally consistent.
- [ ] Update `filter_panel_test.lua`, `ui_format_test.lua`, `ui_layout_test.lua`, and
  `ui_characterization_test.lua` for the empty/default and one-restriction states.

Phase 3 gate:

- [ ] The UI clearly distinguishes unrestricted `All units` from a selected concrete
  restriction, with no multi-select behavior introduced accidentally.

## Phase 4 - Integration, documentation, and packaging

- [ ] Add an integration test proving that empty unit restrictions feed all active
  units into the race provider.
- [ ] Prove that the primary first-open Humanoids race filter still narrows those
  candidates and that removing it exposes all active units.
- [ ] Prove that a scoped Residents/Pets/Visitors window still applies its explicit
  unit restriction and any supplied race restriction.
- [ ] Update README usage text from a Citizens default to the restrictive model and
  explain the separate Humanoids first-open behavior.
- [ ] Update Lua aliases/comments that still describe unit scope as a scalar or an
  inclusive candidate source.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Test.ps1` and record the result below.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Build.ps1` and record the result below.
- [ ] Run `./tools/VerifyPackage.ps1` and record the result below.
- [ ] Perform a live DFHack smoke test:
  - primary window initially displays `All units` but returns humanoids;
  - removing Humanoids exposes non-humanoid active units;
  - choosing and clearing a concrete scope refreshes candidates correctly;
  - each Creatures-menu scoped button opens with its intended restriction.
- [ ] Mark the matching item in `docs/project.todo` complete only after every required
  local validation passes; record any deferred live-only evidence explicitly.

Phase 4 gate:

- [ ] Source, tests, generated package, documentation, and live behavior agree on
  empty-means-all restrictive semantics.

